   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law and the education law, in relation to\nexceptions to requirements for electronic prescriptions; and providing for the\nrepeal of such provisions upon the expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S4183",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4183-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 23,
       "fullName": "Diane J. Savino",
       "imgName": "403_diane_j._savino.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 403,
       "sessionMemberId": 1284,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "SAVINO"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §281, Pub Health L; amd §6810, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4183\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public health law and the educa-\ntion law, in relation to exceptions to requirements for electronic\nprescriptions; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon the\nexpiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo require the mandatory electronic prescribing system accommodate the\nnursing home medication administration system for another two years.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nAmend 281 of the Public Health Law to add nursing home oral\nprescriptions to the exemptions from the electronic prescription\nmandate.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrent law requires all prescriptions for both non-controlled and\ncontrolled substances be prescribed electronically. Since 2016, th e\nDepartment of Health has exempted nursing homes from the mandate, howev-\ner the existing waiver expires in March 24, 2019.\n\nPhysicians and nurse practitioners (NP) are not physically present in\nnursing homes 24 hours a day and resident's medical needs may a rise or\nchange at any time. For decades, the nursing home medication adminis-\ntration system has allowed registered nurses, acting as an agent to the\nphysician or NP, to take orders for medications to be delivered to resi-\ndents safely and without delay. In this long-standing system, the physi-\ncian or NP then routinely signs the oral order within 48 hours.\n\nRecognizing the unique circumstance of nursing homes, the state has\nprovided a waiver to the e-prescribing statute each year. This bill will\nextend the current waiver to March of 2021 in order to prompt the indus-\ntry and Department to resolve the problem.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017-2018: S8943 (Hannon)\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nOctober 21, 2019 and shall expire October 31, 2021.\n",
